17989	{"artifactId":"LLD-93081bff91ae5108-s1","entry":{"id":"migrated-LLD-93081bff91ae5108-s1-review","actor":{"id":"insrc:controller","kind":"agent","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-02T09:33:21.073Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"review: block","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"design.story","stage":"design.story","verdict":"block","findings":[{"claimId":"p1","ref":"2.4 renderPlanBuildRecordMd postconditions","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"`meta.sizeClass` exists on the BuildRecord meta, is present ONLY on records that came through the standalone route, and is NEVER written by the validate path — which is what makes it safe to key the converged renderer's title on, where keying on `meta.standalone` would still flip.","evidence":"Verified, and this was the design's most load-bearing unverified assumption. `readonly sizeClass?: string | undefined` is declared on BuildRecord.meta (standalone-record.ts:49) — OPTIONAL on the wide type and REQUIRED on StandaloneBuildRecord.meta (:94) — so the converged renderer, which takes a BuildRecord, can read it without any type change. The write side confirms the other half: implement.ts:135 writes `standalone: true, sizeClass, ...` on the standalone route, while validate.ts:163's meta literal carries workflow/standalone/epicHash/storyId/createdAt/updatedAt and NO sizeClass — so the validate path cannot clobber it.","action":"none — verified sound. Keying the title on `meta.sizeClass` rather than `meta.standalone` is safe and is exactly what makes the flip harmless even before the second half of the fix lands.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p2","ref":"c3 / migration stateBefore","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"The implement prompt's commit-before-validate instruction lives at src/prompts/build/implement-task.md:26, NOT in the build-step module — this citation was corrected during the ISSUE audit after being wrongly attributed to src/mcp/build-step/render.ts.","evidence":"The corrected citation resolves verbatim: `src/prompts/build/implement-task.md:26` reads `4. If green, **commit** referencing the Task and its issue:`, and the companion phrase is at :41 (`and the commit — then submit this Task to the build validation gate for`). Both patterns return exactly ONE src/ hit each, and both are in that file — nothing in src/mcp/build-step/ matches, confirming the original mis-attribution the ISSUE audit caught.","action":"none — verified sound. The ordering claim the whole defect rests on is anchored where it actually lives.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p3","ref":"3.x dataModelChanges / migration stateBefore","kind":"inventory","severity":"LOW","premise":"There are EXACTLY THREE places a BuildRecord body is written in all of src/ — validate.ts:164, implement.ts:139 and completion-record.ts:44 — and NONE of them sets `summary` or `commit`, which is why both are dead renderer branches.","evidence":"All three reads resolve verbatim: validate.ts:164 `body: { tasks: [{ id: taskId, passed }], ...(changeLog.length > 0 ? { changeLog } : {}) }`, implement.ts:139 `body: { focus: specFocus, producesLld: false }`, completion-record.ts:44 `body: { tasks: [], ...(changeLog.length > 0 ? { changeLog } : {}) }`. The `summary:` and `commit:` greps return 24 and 5 src/ hits respectively and NOT ONE is in a build producer — they are analyze bundles, a git_show description and similar. The zero-producer finding holds by enumeration.","action":"none — verified sound.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p4","ref":"c11 / a1 approach","kind":"external-contract","severity":"LOW","premise":"The `git_diff` builtin ALREADY accepts `from` and `to` range inputs, so deriving a change set from committed history needs no new tool — only a different call. The whole design rests on this: if the builtin were working-tree only, the chosen alternative would require new plumbing.","evidence":"Confirmed verbatim and it is decisive for the design. diff.ts:55 `id: 'git_diff'`, :56 'Supports unstaged (default), staged, arbitrary commit ranges, or a specific path', :62 `from: { type: 'string', description: 'Starting ref for a range diff (commit SHA, branch, tag, \"HEAD~3\").' }`. Range derivation genuinely needs no new capability.","action":"none — verified sound. No alternative could have been rejected on 'that would need new plumbing'.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p5","ref":"c14 / 3.2 BuildRecord.meta.standalone","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"`runValidateSession` is shared by BOTH the plan-driven and the standalone validate branches and writes `meta.standalone: false` UNCONDITIONALLY, which combined with mergeWithPrior's `{...rec.meta}` spread makes the renderer flip reachable rather than theoretical.","evidence":"The renderer-flip is confirmed reachable on four independent anchors: validate.ts:72 `if (input.standalone !== undefined) {` routing to handleStandaloneValidate (:73, declared :91); BOTH branches converging on runValidateSession (:80 for the standalone branch, :116 for the plan-driven one); the shared function's own comment at :121 stating it serves 'BOTH the plan-driven and the standalone (S002) branches so the verdict + persist behaviour is identical'; and :163 writing `standalone: false` unconditionally inside it.","action":"none — verified sound. The ISSUE flagged this as unconfirmed and it is now established from the code, which is what promoted it from a caveat to a design driver.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p6","ref":"c7 / invariantsToPreserve","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"`mergeWithPrior` constructs meta as `{ ...rec.meta, createdAt: prior.meta.createdAt, ... }`, so the NEW write wins on every meta field except the explicitly preserved ones — which is both why the flip happens today and why omitting `standalone` from the new write will let the prior value survive.","evidence":"standalone-record.ts:246 declares mergeWithPrior, :251 is `...rec.meta` and :252 `createdAt: prior.meta.createdAt`. The new write wins on every meta field except the explicitly preserved ones, exactly as the design states — which is simultaneously why the flip happens today and why omitting the flag will let the prior value survive.","action":"none — verified sound. The fix leans on existing merge semantics rather than changing them, which is the lower-risk direction.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p7","ref":"c5 / a4 rationale","kind":"inventory","severity":"LOW","premise":"`renderStandaloneBuildRecordMd` emits ONLY the title, the size-class/created line, `## Scope` and an optional `## Triage rationale` — it reads none of changeLog, feedback or summary. This is the asymmetry that makes a trivial-routed build structurally incapable of showing provenance.","evidence":"standalone-record.ts:114 declares renderStandaloneBuildRecordMd; :116 emits `# Build (standalone ${rec.meta.sizeClass}) — Story ${rec.meta.storyId}`; :120 `'## Scope'`; :124 the optional `'## Triage rationale'`. The `'## Changes'`, `'## Feedback'`, `'## Summary'` and `'## Tasks validated'` literals appear ONLY in the plan-driven renderer (:152, :163, :167) and never in this one. The asymmetry is exactly as described.","action":"none — verified sound.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p8","ref":"c6 / persistBuildRecord postconditions","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"`persistBuildRecord` selects the renderer on `merged.meta.standalone` — a ternary between renderStandaloneBuildRecordMd and renderPlanBuildRecordMd — which is the single dispatch the chosen alternative removes.","evidence":"standalone-record.ts:198 `const md = merged.meta.standalone` and :200 `: renderPlanBuildRecordMd(merged);` — one ternary, one dispatch, which is the single point the convergence removes.","action":"none — verified sound.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p9","ref":"a2 rejection / c12","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"The code-review grounding assembler already implements a working-tree-first derivation that falls back to `git_diff {from:'HEAD^'}` and then to the root commit via git's empty-tree object — the shipped precedent this design examines and deliberately declines to adopt.","evidence":"grounding.ts:154 carries the comment 'falls back to the last commit (`git_diff {from:'HEAD^'}`,', :166-167 declares `EMPTY_TREE_HASH = '4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904'` with the single-commit-repo rationale, and :298 actually uses it: `return await runDiff(repoPath, { from: EMPTY_TREE_HASH });`. The precedent exists and is live, not aspirational — which is what makes declining to adopt it a real decision rather than ignorance of it.","action":"none — verified sound. The rejection reasoning stands on an accurate reading of what the precedent does.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p10","ref":"c13 / testStrategy framework","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"A gated throwaway-repo integration test pattern already exists in the code-review suite — it creates a real repository and skips cleanly when git is unavailable — and is the pattern this Story's decisive tests extend rather than invent.","evidence":"diff-grounding.test.ts:148 is the named integration test and :150 is the gate verbatim: `try { execFileSync('git', ['--version'], { stdio: 'ignore' }); } catch { git = false; }`. The pattern the test strategy extends exists and is committed.","action":"none — verified sound.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p11","ref":"c1 / 2.1 changedFiles","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"`changedFiles` today derives the set STRICTLY from the working tree — it loops over `staged` false and true calling the git_diff builtin and unions the paths — with no commit-range path at all.","evidence":"changed-files.ts:43 is `export async function changedFiles(repoPath: string): Promise<readonly string[]> {` — note it currently takes ONLY repoPath, which confirms the proposed options argument is genuinely additive rather than a signature change. :46 is the `for (const staged of [false, true])` loop and :47 the gitDiffTool call. No commit-range path exists today.","action":"none — verified sound.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p12","ref":"c9 / invariantsToPreserve","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"`changeLogBodyLines` returns an empty array for BOTH an absent and an empty change log, which is why the missing `## Changes` section is silent rather than signalled.","evidence":"bindings.ts:91-92 verbatim: `export function changeLogBodyLines(changeLog: ChangeLog | undefined): string[] {` / `if (changeLog === undefined || changeLog.length === 0) return [];`. Absent and empty are treated identically, which is why the missing section is silent.","action":"none — verified sound.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p13","ref":"backwardCompat","kind":"inventory","severity":"MED","premise":"`renderStandaloneBuildRecordMd` has only the call sites inside standalone-record.ts itself, so retaining it as a delegating shim is sufficient for backward compatibility — no external module calls it directly.","evidence":"The claim is WRONG as worded and the correction is actionable. I asserted renderStandaloneBuildRecordMd has 'only the call sites inside standalone-record.ts itself' and that 'no external module calls it directly'. The grep finds 5 src/ hits, and two are in a TEST that calls it directly: build-record.test.ts:28 imports it and :142 asserts `assert.equal(readFileSync(md, 'utf8'), renderStandaloneBuildRecordMd(rec));` — i.e. an existing committed test asserts that the WRITTEN markdown for a standalone record equals what that function returns.","action":"This is good news badly stated, and the plan stage should treat it as a gift rather than a problem. That existing assertion IS the byte-identity guard the design says must be built: it already pins 'what persistBuildRecord wrote' against 'what the standalone renderer returns'. After convergence it keeps passing ONLY if the delegating shim and the single renderer produce identical bytes for a standalone record — exactly the k2 constraint. So migration step 2 should NOT invent a new fixture from scratch; it should first run this test and treat it as the baseline, then add the real-pre-fix-record fixture for the cases it does not cover. Correct the backwardCompat wording too: the shim is retained because a committed TEST calls it, which is a stronger reason than the speculative external caller I gave.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The claim overstated the isolation of the symbol. The substance of the decision (retain the shim) is unchanged and in fact better supported, but the design's own test strategy should be pointed at an existing guard instead of duplicating it."}},{"claimId":"p14","ref":"backwardCompat / invariantsToPreserve","kind":"inventory","severity":"LOW","premise":"`collectBuildChangeLog` has exactly TWO non-test call sites — the validate phase and the completion-time writer — so widening its context additively affects a bounded, enumerable set of callers.","evidence":"The two non-test call sites resolve verbatim: validate.ts:161 `const changeLog = await collectBuildChangeLog(repoPath, { author: 'insrc-build', timestamp: now });` and completion-record.ts:40 `? await collectBuildChangeLog(repoPath, { author: 'insrc-build', timestamp: now }, listChanged)`. The remaining src/ hits are in changed-files.test.ts. A bounded, enumerable caller set, as claimed.","action":"none — verified sound. Note the completion-time site already passes the optional third argument, so the seam widening has a precedent in its own caller.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p15","ref":"backwardCompat / StandaloneBuildRecord","kind":"cross-artifact","severity":"LOW","premise":"The code-review subject consumes a `buildRecord`, which is why the design retains `StandaloneBuildRecord` as a type rather than deleting it — the convergence must not break that consumer.","evidence":"Confirmed, and STRONGER than the design claimed. code-review/types.ts:110 declares `readonly buildRecord: StandaloneBuildRecord | null;` — the code-review subject is typed against the NARROW shape, not BuildRecord. So retaining StandaloneBuildRecord is not merely prudent, it is REQUIRED for that module to compile. (The raw grep for the type name returned 0 src/ hits only because its 50-match cap was exhausted by docs/; the typed read settles it.)","action":"none blocking — but worth carrying into the plan as an observation the design did not make. A record flowing into code review is typed as StandaloneBuildRecord while actually holding plan-driven content (tasks, changeLog), so that consumer is already looking at records through a type that does not declare the fields they carry. Converging the shapes would IMPROVE this rather than threaten it, which strengthens the chosen alternative's case; the design currently frames the code-review consumer only as a compatibility cost.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p17","ref":"2.4 renderPlanBuildRecordMd postconditions","kind":"closed-union","severity":"LOW","premise":"The converged renderer's section list is complete at nine members — title, created/updated line, Commit, Scope, Triage rationale, Summary, Tasks validated, Changes, Feedback — i.e. the union of what the two existing renderers emit today, with nothing dropped from either.","evidence":"All nine sections resolve to real emit sites in standalone-record.ts: the standalone title at :116, `'## Scope'` at :120, `'## Triage rationale'` at :124, `**Commit:**` at :140, `'## Summary'` at :148, `'## Tasks validated'` at :152, `'## Changes'` at :163, `'## Feedback'` at :167. The union is complete and nothing from either renderer is dropped. (`'## Scope'` also matches render.ts:166, which is the build PROMPT's own section and unrelated.)","action":"none — verified sound. The converged section list is the true union of both renderers.","fixability":"manual"}],"counts":{"high":0,"med":1,"low":15},"reviewedAt":"2026-10-02T09:33:21.073Z","model":"client"},"resolutions":{"p13":{"findingId":"p13","status":"resolved","note":"Resolved by correcting the LLD in five places (edits made by hand; the finding carried a rationale but no artifactEdits). The claim was wrong: renderStandaloneBuildRecordMd HAS an external caller — build-record.test.ts:28 imports it and :142 calls it. The review action text was ALSO wrong in the other direction, and that is the substantive correction: it said the existing assertion \"keeps passing ONLY if the shim and the single renderer produce identical bytes\". It does not. Once renderStandaloneBuildRecordMd is a delegating shim, assert.equal(readFileSync(md), renderStandaloneBuildRecordMd(rec)) has the converged renderer on BOTH sides — persistBuildRecord writes through it and the shim resolves to it — so the assertion is a tautology that passes for any output, including one that changes every standalone byte. Treating it as a ready-made k2 guard would have shipped a green check that guards nothing. Corrections applied: (1) §2.5 Returns now names the committed test as the concrete reason the shim is retained and records that s1 was wrong; (2) §2.5 gains a postcondition documenting the self-comparison hazard; (3) §5 contract level gains an explicit subject to CONVERT build-record.test.ts:142 to a frozen golden, with a mutation check that it goes red; (4) §6 step 2 folds that conversion in and requires the golden be captured BEFORE step 7, since a golden captured after records post-fix bytes; (5) §6 Backward compat is reworded, and additionally now states that StandaloneBuildRecord retention is REQUIRED (code-review/types.ts:110 types buildRecord as StandaloneBuildRecord | null) rather than merely prudent, noting the pre-existing looseness convergence would improve.","resolvedAt":"2026-10-02T09:37:08.913Z"}}}}}}
